WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.319 --> 00:00:08.370
[Waves crashing]
The mighty song of the ocean.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:13.589
Beneath the waves, the story of a young whale’s
astonishing voyage across uncharted waters

00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:14.950
is about to begin.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:19.700
But this is not Earth’s ocean, nor is this
an Earth whale.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:25.189
This is the saga of “South Scrimshaw,”
a game-documentary hybrid created by the brilliant

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:27.070
Nathan O. Marsh.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:32.390
In this video, we’ll follow the young whale’s
odyssey through the alien ocean depths, and

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:35.760
discover a tale of loss, and deep connections.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.530
And you can play the full game yourself and
support the artist using the links below.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:50.320
Now, the sea is calling…

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.220
The journey of a lifetime begins in the comforting
warmth of an aquatic egg.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:59.679
A newborn mind is getting their very first
look at sunlight through the membrane of their

00:00:59.679 --> 00:01:01.050
sanctuary.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:06.020
This is a male whale calf — not the sort
of creature one might expect to find incubating

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.380
inside an egg.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:11.820
But this is no ordinary egg, and this is no
ordinary calf.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.160
And his extraordinary life is about to start.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:23.409
A thunderous call reverberates from the unknown
world beyond the egg, shaking the calf to

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:24.409
his core.

00:01:24.409 --> 00:01:26.160
Yet the calf isn’t frightened.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.620
He recognizes the call as his mother’s.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:33.899
Instincts tell him to stretch his new body
for the first time, pushing against the membrane

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:34.899
of the egg.

00:01:34.899 --> 00:01:37.009
A seam begins to open.

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:41.969
Just one more impressive stretch, and the
only world he’s ever known opens into a

00:01:41.969 --> 00:01:44.910
blinding light!

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:50.470
Warm, tropical shallows flourish on the other
side of the egg.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:54.820
To be completely technical, this is not an
‘egg’ as we’d understand it, but a kind

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:58.979
of external womb that local scientists call
a “birthing shell.”

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:02.850
Like most mammals on our planet, the whales
we know give live birth.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.079
But we are no longer on our planet.

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:09.190
At last, the calf enters the teeming waters.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:13.800
A bewildering array of life overwhelms his
delicate senses.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.040
Tiny fish waste no time in gobbling flecks
of nutrients.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.050
Foliage seem to react to our calf’s presence,
clinging to him like they have a mind of their

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:24.050
own.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.920
Even with his mother’s reassuring call,
this is all too much for the calf.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:34.340
He swims downwards, passing bizarre sponges
and crustaceans that dwell amidst the fronds.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.210
Something gently puts a stop to his panicked
dive.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:40.120
Our calf is being carried to the surface.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.200
The upward motion is tender, like the steady
movements of a cradle.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.200
The young whale has no idea what to make of
any of this.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.890
At last, he breaches, and enjoys his first
critical breath of air.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.319
Like earth whales, he can’t breathe underwater,
and might have drowned without intervention.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:02.629
Now, the calf finds himself in an empty expanse.

00:03:02.629 --> 00:03:07.640
In an ordinary season, this region would be
filled with whales just like himself.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:14.800
Instead, the surface is woefully empty — with
the unusual exception of a large mass of plants.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.580
Our calf stares at the peculiar island.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.260
The plant fronds continue to tenderly grasp
at him.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.700
None of his instincts are any help.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.989
Something moves atop the heap of plant matter.

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:29.640
What is it?

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:34.459
[bird call] Nothing good, the infant decides.

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:36.989
The surface world is much too strange.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:42.640
Diving back under the surface, the poor calf
is overwhelmed with fear and hunger.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:46.390
He knows he needs to nurse, but can’t locate
his mother.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:49.950
The plants ensnare him once again, as if detecting
helplessness.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:56.360
But though only minutes old, our calf is still
able to fight for survival, biting the vines.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:59.040
His mother’s comforting call makes him stop.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.260
The remarkable truth finally dawns on our
calf.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:07.270
The mass of foliage is his mother, and she
has been with him all along.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:14.420
She is a Brillo Whale at a remarkable later
stage of life — a window into the calf’s

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.220
astonishing future.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:25.449
A suspicious pile of rock lies on the sandy
floor of the Brillo breeding grounds.

00:04:25.449 --> 00:04:27.920
This warrants a closer investigation.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:33.610
Time for a deep scan.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.580
Just as we suspected — it’s a Stonedog.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:41.690
This unique animal lives in a shell of scavenged
material, much like the nomadic hermit crabs

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:43.190
of Earth’s beaches.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:48.040
The Stonedog is trying to hide, but is it
a convincing form of camouflage?

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.389
The creature checks to make sure it hasn’t
been spotted.

00:04:51.389 --> 00:04:53.449
Too late.

00:04:53.449 --> 00:04:58.630
Our calf is looking for fun, not food, but
the Stonedog doesn’t know this.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:01.810
Our calf has grown curious and rambunctious.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:05.210
His tumultuous birth hasn’t hindered his
playfulness.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:10.979
He enjoys lifting his fluke into the air —something
Earth whales do for a variety of reasons,

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:13.180
including showing off.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:18.319
Light rain has begun to fall, a phenomenon
completely novel to the young calf.

00:05:18.319 --> 00:05:21.660
A bellow from his mother reminds him not to
wander off.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:27.180
Though eager for adventure, the little calf
is still much too anxious to leave her side.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:33.100
He excitedly shows his mother what he found
exploring, by bumping it into her face.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.140
The mother is trying to rest.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.319
But her love for her son is stronger than
her weariness.

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:41.479
And this playing is not mere frivolity.

00:05:41.479 --> 00:05:47.310
For cetaceans like whales and dolphins, play
is a vital part of cognitive development — serving

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.509
as important training for catching food.

00:05:49.509 --> 00:05:54.389
But right now, there’s only one type of
food our whale calf cares about.

00:05:54.389 --> 00:05:59.660
His mother’s milk will provide the nourishment
needed to grow a special layer of spongy tissue

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:02.350
that makes Brillo Whales so unique.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:07.860
In adult Brillo Whales, this tissue acts as
a refuge for other organisms, allowing for

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:11.069
symbiosis beyond anything here on Earth.

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:16.280
The mother has chosen to bond with botanical
matter, and while she might appear overrun,

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.060
the truth is far more interesting.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:25.870
Another deep scan will reveal how plant and
animal have merged to an extraordinary degree.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:30.639
Unlike the stone dog, the foliage and small
animal life surrounding the mother whale’s

00:06:30.639 --> 00:06:34.050
body aren’t just camouflage, but a way of
life.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:39.160
Like a kind of living hotel, her body has
designated areas where other lifeforms can

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:44.990
take up residence, with a secondary layer
protecting her vitals from her many houseguests.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:49.430
But this bizarre anatomy leaves us with more
questions than answers.

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:53.610
Most notably — what is the benefit of such
a peculiar system?

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:58.849
To help us visualize the explanation, we’ll
get a little help… from a seabunny, better

00:06:58.849 --> 00:07:00.669
known as a seabun.

00:07:00.669 --> 00:07:04.860
The seabun is a mascot that has long been
associated with this planet…

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.770
however, it is not a real animal, even in
this world.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:13.949
The seabun myth originates from the early
days of planetary exploration, and was something

00:07:13.949 --> 00:07:16.910
of an urban legend among the first scientists.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:22.629
Over the years, the joke took on a life of
its own, with some people mistakenly believing

00:07:22.629 --> 00:07:25.510
the seabun to be an actual animal.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:30.690
Yet the seabun will serve as a helpful example
for why an organism might want to bond with

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:31.950
a whale.

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:36.880
Underwater locomotion is energy intensive,
and ‘carpooling’ with another lifeform

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:38.730
can be a great benefit.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:43.520
Living on a huge whale also means predators
might think twice about getting close.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.330
In return, some animals that partner with
Brillos will help them hunt for food.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:53.240
Some Brillo partners will even feed directly
off the whales themselves, in return for some

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.970
sort of useful service.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:08:00.240
It might sound like too strange a partnership
to emerge, but we can find analogous relationships

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.629
right here on Earth.

00:08:01.629 --> 00:08:05.879
Oxpeckers are birds that clean parasites off
the side of large mammals.

00:08:05.879 --> 00:08:11.259
During the process, the oxpecker skims a marginal
amount of the mammal’s blood as a sort of

00:08:11.259 --> 00:08:12.840
nutritional bonus.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:18.090
The large animals put up with this, as the
parasite removal service is worth the cost.

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:23.759
But Brillos have taken symbiosis to new extremes,
to the point where many partner species have

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:27.220
evolved specifically to live their lives around
the whales.

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:32.469
Sometimes, the two lifeforms become so interdependent
they’re difficult to tell apart — but

00:08:32.469 --> 00:08:34.669
such cases are thankfully uncommon.

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:38.910
So, what kind of a relationship does the mother
have with her plants?

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:41.919
The answer has to do with photosynthesis.

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:47.290
On her own, the mother has no way of extracting
energy from the sun, but plants do it all

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:51.930
the time, and they share some of the sugars
they produce with their generous host.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:56.310
The tradeoff is that she now must spend a
considerable amount of time near the surface,

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:59.120
where there’s enough sunlight for the plants
to grow.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:04.070
Emerald Sea Slugs of Earth have a similar
partnership with algae that grows within their

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:05.070
bodies.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.709
However, not all passengers are welcome.

00:09:07.709 --> 00:09:09.430
This is a Blood Trilobite.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:12.510
They aren’t here to pay their fair share.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:18.070
The Blood Trilobite is a bandit, pilfering
from a vein with an extended proboscis.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:21.420
Fortunately, the mother has a personal bodyguard.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:27.180
The Vellum Worm has one duty in this ecosystem
— to remove uninvited guests.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:32.390
It’s a busy job, as herbivores also seek
to feed on her many leaves.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:34.959
A hungry fish thinks they’ve hit the jackpot.

00:09:34.959 --> 00:09:37.580
They’re in for a rude awakening.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:43.149
The Vellum Worm has a neurotoxin-dispensing
barb to make sure gatecrashers get the message.

00:09:43.149 --> 00:09:46.930
The misfortunate fish stands little chance.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:50.399
The Brillo Whale’s ecosystem is called a
‘Symbiotic Garden.’

00:09:50.399 --> 00:09:55.370
But like much of the life on this planet,
these plants seem too Earth-like to have evolved

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:56.900
independently.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:00.110
Just how did such life come to this alien
ocean?

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:02.810
That’s a question that will have to wait.

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:08.410
Right now, our calf has discovered his mother’s
plants are fun to play with — but she scolds

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:09.410
him.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:13.600
Such behavior is currently too risky — for
the mother is quite unwell.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.390
A sharp branch has punctured through her protective
layer.

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:21.600
These kinds of complications can merge in
Brillo Whales as they get older.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.560
The thorns are in danger of injuring her intestines.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.079
A sudden movement could mean the end.

00:10:27.079 --> 00:10:31.980
The mother does not know how long she will
be around to protect her calf, and is determined

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:35.640
to reach the safety of the Brillo herd before
the situation worsens.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:40.022
A long journey lies ahead of them, but for
now, mother and child take a moment to rest

00:10:40.022 --> 00:10:41.022
together…

00:10:41.022 --> 00:10:45.639
To understand the voyage ahead, let’s examine
a simplified map of the surrounding ocean.

00:10:45.639 --> 00:10:51.040
The dark blue line represents an ocean fault,
which pushed up land from the sea, forming

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:53.339
the Chartreuse Archipelago.

00:10:53.339 --> 00:10:58.730
The first human explorers named each island
in the chain, including ‘South Scrimshaw’

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.760
where this documentary gets its title.

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:06.310
Thus far, the mother and son have lived in
the safety of the nearby region of Phthalo

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:07.310
Bay.

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:12.010
But they have traveled all the way to here
— and are about to cross into dangerous

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:14.470
open ocean.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:16.860
It is the dawn of a new day.

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:21.610
The mother swims under the surface, making
mysterious calls through the deep.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:27.070
The calf returns from surfacing, retreating
to the safety of the bramble beneath his mother.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:31.770
He doesn’t understand the purpose of her
calls, but he recognizes she is on alert — and

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:35.300
that the waters before them are deeper than
any he’s known.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:38.560
A sound comes from the far distance.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.269
It’s the call of another Brillo whale, approaching
their position.

00:11:43.269 --> 00:11:46.180
Our calf is curious about the new arrival.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:50.579
He spots a far-off speck, and watches it grow
steadily larger.

00:11:50.579 --> 00:11:54.620
Even as the other whale gets closer, the calf
seems uncertain.

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:57.279
Something about this other whale isn’t right.

00:11:57.279 --> 00:12:00.019
The calf retreats further into the bramble
nest.

00:12:00.019 --> 00:12:05.029
The mother admonishes him in response, as
if encouraging him to be more neighborly.

00:12:05.029 --> 00:12:07.329
His apprehension is understandable.

00:12:07.329 --> 00:12:10.389
For this Brillo whale is in a clever disguise.

00:12:10.389 --> 00:12:15.680
A false head is located on the creature’s
tail, in an attempt at mimicking a threatening

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:16.680
predator.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:20.950
The Brillo has no way to construct such a
costume on its own — but this Brillo has

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:23.470
a unique symbiotic assistant.

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:25.230
A Weaverlobe.

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:30.320
These astute designers craft intimidating
attire for their whale mobile-homes to deter

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:31.320
attackers.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:36.779
They spin the apparel from a material called
ossomer, a unique kind of long-chain polymer.

00:12:36.779 --> 00:12:41.709
While the animal kingdom is full of clever
architects, it seems unlikely that a single

00:12:41.709 --> 00:12:44.699
organism could create something so complex.

00:12:44.699 --> 00:12:50.510
Instead, it is likely that a colony of Weaverlobes
worked together to craft the impressive camouflage.

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:55.310
In any case, this Brillo will be a powerful
ally for the coming journey.

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:57.530
And she is not alone.

00:12:57.530 --> 00:13:01.820
Like our mother, she is looking to transport
her calf to safety.

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:04.190
Our calf still isn’t feeling very welcoming.

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:07.110
And he’s in for another surprise visitor.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:10.620
A strange, rocky shape approaches from behind.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:12.100
It’s another whale.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:17.621
The armored Brillo is also a mother, and seems
to be suffering from an internal injury: only

00:13:17.621 --> 00:13:19.600
able to produce short honks.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:25.019
While the calf is uncomfortable, these traveling
companions will make for a safer crossing.

00:13:25.019 --> 00:13:26.889
His mother nudges him from above.

00:13:26.889 --> 00:13:28.980
It’s time to depart.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:32.460
The deep ocean awaits.

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:37.089
Only fifty-seven kilometers now separate the
pod from the Great Southern Reef.

00:13:37.089 --> 00:13:42.360
This final stage of the voyage takes them
over a treacherous abyssal trench, so the

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:45.290
mothers swim in defensive formation.

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:49.850
With just three adults, however, they cannot
completely protect their calves.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:54.470
Our youngster has done an admirable job keeping
up with their hurried pace.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:58.079
He too can sense there is no time to linger.

00:13:58.079 --> 00:13:59.220
Something is out there.

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:03.450
A dark shadow has been following the pod since
the early afternoon.

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:08.800
Though it has yet to attack, the unknown pursuer
seems to be biding its time.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:12.699
The calf no longer finds the other Brillos
quite so frightening.

00:14:12.699 --> 00:14:17.490
To his left, the stony mother and her child
swim in a protective arrangement.

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:20.709
This calf seems just as uneasy as ours is.

00:14:20.709 --> 00:14:26.459
The exterior of the stony mother has reduced
in size to become more streamlined, shrinking

00:14:26.459 --> 00:14:29.990
via some unknown internal mechanism.

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:34.199
Even when in exposed waters, Brillos are full
of surprises.

00:14:34.199 --> 00:14:38.089
Our calf doesn’t understand the stony mother’s
changed appearance.

00:14:38.089 --> 00:14:43.150
He turns his attention to down below, where
the mimic mother swims with her calf.

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:47.060
Alongside a light from one of the documentary
crew’s drones.

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:51.079
Since the light has already interrupted the
shot, this seems like a natural moment to

00:14:51.079 --> 00:14:55.740
discuss the technology used in the documentation
of Brillo whales.

00:14:55.740 --> 00:15:00.459
The scientists who have come to this planet
primarily use repurposed drones in the filming

00:15:00.459 --> 00:15:01.779
of wildlife.

00:15:01.779 --> 00:15:06.279
The inner-workings of these drones are quite
sophisticated, allowing them to keep pace

00:15:06.279 --> 00:15:10.730
with aquatic life without disrupting their
natural routines.

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:16.000
Returning to the scene, the mimic mother swims
upside down to prevent attacks from below.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:21.959
She’s uneasy about her role in the formation,
as her disguise isn’t effective from this

00:15:21.959 --> 00:15:22.959
angle.

00:15:22.959 --> 00:15:24.889
Our calf seems concerned as well.

00:15:24.889 --> 00:15:27.450
His mother reassures him with a hum.

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:29.069
He is safe with her.

00:15:29.069 --> 00:15:30.279
But the shadow remains.

00:15:30.279 --> 00:15:34.860
It’s a Penumbra Shark, the fastest predator
in this ocean.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:38.880
It could close the gap between itself and
the whales at any time.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:40.370
There’s no rush.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:45.139
Like the lightning-fast sailfish of Earth’s
oceans, the Penumbra Shark possesses a mighty

00:15:45.139 --> 00:15:46.420
dorsal fin.

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:50.760
They keep it withdrawn — until ready to
strike.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:52.769
In an instant, the shark has vanished.

00:15:52.769 --> 00:15:56.230
In a panic, the mothers break from their defensive
formation.

00:15:56.230 --> 00:16:00.269
Our calf glances down — and sees nothing
but open ocean.

00:16:00.269 --> 00:16:04.259
He is completely exposed to attack.

00:16:04.259 --> 00:16:08.580
Instantaneously, adrenaline floods his system.

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:13.350
His brain goes into overdrive, now taking
in every horrible detail.

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:16.980
The monster’s teeth shine like stoplights
through the darkness.

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:19.930
Our calf cannot possibly escape in time.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.080
But his mother has a strategy.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:27.110
Tension resides in her thorny branches, that
can be released with whip-like speed.

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:31.920
One forceful snap, and her child is protected
in a fortification of brambles.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:37.670
But the shark detects the shift - and changes
targets - to the stone mother’s calf - who

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:40.070
is woefully exposed.

00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:41.759
Our calf looks on in horror.

00:16:41.759 --> 00:16:48.769
The sight is nearly too traumatic to process.

00:16:48.769 --> 00:16:54.600
It is only due to the vigilance of his mother
that our calf survived the encounter.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:58.529
The safety of the reef and the other Brillos
are now very close.

00:16:58.529 --> 00:17:01.279
The mimic mother pulls ahead of the pod.

00:17:01.279 --> 00:17:06.929
The stone mother follows, disoriented and
in shock after losing her only child.

00:17:06.929 --> 00:17:10.230
Our calf is falling further and further behind.

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:14.560
He does not wish to leave his mother, and
she is not keeping pace.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.160
He swims back to her, trying to coax her to
continue.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:20.130
But she is no longer moving.

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:25.730
Saving her calf came at the sacrifice of reopening
her preexisting injuries.

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:31.539
Not understanding, the child calls to his
mother as he has a hundred times before.

00:17:31.539 --> 00:17:33.150
There is no response.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:37.610
She has done all she can to protect her son,
but she can hold on no longer.

00:17:37.610 --> 00:17:40.240
Still, our calf prods his mother to move.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:46.700
At this age, our calf has no hope of surviving
alone without a mother’s milk and protection.

00:17:46.700 --> 00:17:48.730
This could be the end…

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:52.220
…But another call echoes through the water.

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:56.299
The stone mother is circling back.

00:17:56.299 --> 00:17:59.340
A new day begins on the Great Southern Reef.

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:03.470
The stone mother has arrived safely… and
she is not alone.

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:07.470
Our calf drinks its fill of milk, recovering
from the arduous journey.

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:10.799
It is a moving case of social adoption.

00:18:10.799 --> 00:18:15.650
The stone mother’s exterior has now deflated
to the point where we can see she is a young

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:18.120
adult, quite small for her species.

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:22.990
She has rescued our orphaned calf from certain
doom, and perhaps the calf has rescued her

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:24.179
as well.

00:18:24.179 --> 00:18:29.210
Physically and emotionally exhausted, the
new family gets some much-needed rest.

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:36.740
In the morning, they will wake to an entirely
new world.

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:39.830
Warm sunlight illuminates the coral of the
Great Reef.

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:44.040
An inquisitive Bikili fish picks through the
irregular terrain.

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:47.480
She’s hungry, and thinks she’s spotted
breakfast.

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:51.870
The minute eyes of Greebles peer from the
alcoves below.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:55.789
The Bikili fish has her work cut out for her
trying to dig them out.

00:18:55.789 --> 00:18:59.260
But before she can begin, she is rudely chased
off.

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:02.390
A larger hunter is throwing their weight around.

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:06.970
This is a spotted barnacle claw, and it will
have no trouble catching Greebles.

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:12.410
A single, forceful tug wrenches the entire
colony from the nooks below.

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:17.340
The host Brillo Whale isn’t satisfied with
its partner species’ choice of food.

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:19.880
It needs something a bit meatier.

00:19:19.880 --> 00:19:24.620
Our young whale witnesses the unfamiliar spectacle
from a distance.

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:28.900
Even after some time living on the reef, there
is always something new to see.

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:32.100
The bellows of other Brillos reverberate through
the water.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:35.780
Though many are strangers, there is at least
one familiar face.

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:42.030
Though her appearance has changed considerably,
his adoptive mother never strays too far away.

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:47.990
Many eye spots now disguise her exterior — likely
a response to the Penumbra shark attack.

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:51.570
And though he might not look it, our whale
has changed too.

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:57.220
His body has at last developed the necessary
tissue layer to bond with other species.

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:59.750
But what kind of adult will he become?

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:03.260
It’s the most important question in any
young Brillo’s life.

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:07.500
There are near unlimited combinations, and
the wrong choice can mean disaster.

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:11.520
A common strategy is to imitate successful
adults.

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:16.780
A young Brillo finds a prosperous grownup,
and attempts to follow in their footsteps.

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:21.539
Adults will sometimes take apprentice Brillos
under their flippers for multiple years, providing

00:20:21.539 --> 00:20:25.460
them with the materials needed to complete
a transformation.

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:28.240
Not all body-plans are equally appealing.

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:33.510
This old veteran is welcoming, but our whale
isn’t sure it’s the career for him.

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:38.190
It’s an understandable feeling, although
another Brillo might be more open to the job

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:39.330
offer.

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:44.340
More than half of Brillos choose to replicate
their biological mothers, and our whale seems

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:47.170
intent on preserving her legacy.

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:51.570
This botanical mass is made of the same plants
his mother bonded with.

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:56.980
But this Brillo has long passed, and is now
nothing but an overgrown graveyard.

00:20:56.980 --> 00:21:01.590
Though our whale cannot bond with the decayed
plants, they still hold a profound emotional

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:02.590
significance.

00:21:02.590 --> 00:21:08.710
Our whale needs to bond soon, but is having
trouble finding a living plant-based Brillo.

00:21:08.710 --> 00:21:12.820
There is one expectation — an adult with
a stunning cloak of foliage.

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:18.320
But despite repeated attempts from the youngster,
this adult doesn’t seem interested in taking

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:19.920
on an apprentice.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:25.150
He is a lone male — a bachelor looking for
a mate, not a babysitting gig.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:28.840
His latest attempt to find love is a dismal
failure.

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:33.690
She seems confused by his little shadow, who
yanks at his well-groomed coat.

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:38.360
It’s the final straw — our whale is no
longer welcome.

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:43.840
The intricacy of Brillo whale’s Symbiotic
Gardens is impressive, but not entirely unheard

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:44.840
of.

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:50.190
A single adult human can have over 39 trillion
microbial cells living inside their body at

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:51.190
a time.

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:57.150
Not just germs, these foreign microorganisms
play a vital role in our survival.

00:21:57.150 --> 00:22:01.760
The Brillos just take this concept further
by integrating with macro-organisms.

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:07.410
There are numerous species our whale could
merge with, even if plants are off the menu.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:10.500
This reef is a never-ending menagerie of life.

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:15.210
The reason for this abundance has to do with
the structure of the coral itself.

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:20.120
The Great Southern Reef is a fringing reef,
comprised of multiple layers.

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:23.510
Spire Coral is the reason for the unique structure.

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:29.059
This distinct species starts small, then grows
steadily until it becomes a towering mass

00:22:29.059 --> 00:22:31.419
that other coral grows on top of.

00:22:31.419 --> 00:22:37.110
With time, the central mass is smothered,
and the structure becomes over-encumbered.

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:43.510
The tower then shatters, collapsing, with
new Spire Coral growing from the fallen giant.

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:49.890
This endless cycle has created an environment
of innumerable ravines and hide-aways.

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:54.789
Organisms like saltwater tadpoles thrive in
this coral city, spending their early stages

00:22:54.789 --> 00:22:59.570
safely hidden away, before emerging as mighty
carnivores.

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:02.529
Well, relatively mighty.

00:23:02.529 --> 00:23:04.290
Time marches ever onwards.

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:10.120
Our young whale has seen many forms of adulthood,
some of which are completely baffling.

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:13.010
Once again, he isn’t interested.

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:18.300
One afternoon, his fortunes seem to turn when
an impressive hunting party reappears from

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:19.300
the wild.

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:22.360
The head of the pod calls to our youngster.

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:23.770
He doesn’t respond.

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:29.600
This adult is a brilliant huntsman, partnering
with the Chowper, a rather unintelligent shark

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:33.710
species who sees him as a pack member, and
follows his lead.

00:23:33.710 --> 00:23:38.830
It’s an incredible strategy, and this adult
is ready to share the tricks of the trade.

00:23:38.830 --> 00:23:41.180
But our whale still isn’t moving.

00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:45.230
All his attention is on the Chowper.

00:23:45.230 --> 00:23:48.840
His trauma is too severe to partner with a
shark species.

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:50.610
He flees.

00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:54.580
Over the coming weeks, our whale’s mental
health continues to spiral.

00:23:54.580 --> 00:24:00.160
It is crucial for his survival that he starts
symbiosis, but he’s too unwell to begin.

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:06.320
The agitated youth can sense that time is
short, but this stress is little help.

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:08.580
More adult Brillos come and go.

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:12.419
This camouflaged whale is one of the more
unique that has appeared on the reef.

00:24:12.419 --> 00:24:17.450
It is covered in octopuses that have linked
themselves together — allowing them to instantly

00:24:17.450 --> 00:24:20.250
change their colors in unison.

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:24.510
Octopuses in our oceans have similar color
shifting abilities, although they pale in

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:29.169
comparison to the color changing abilities
of their interplanetary counterparts.

00:24:29.169 --> 00:24:33.860
But the time has come at last to answer the
question of why life on this planet seems

00:24:33.860 --> 00:24:36.049
so similar to life on Earth.

00:24:36.049 --> 00:24:40.730
One might assume the answer is convergent
evolution — a process by which different

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:46.000
species evolve analogous features due to comparable
evolutionary pressures.

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:48.200
But this answer is not sufficient.

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:52.900
The true reason why Brillo whales look so
much like Earth whales is that they share

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:54.890
a common ancestor.

00:24:54.890 --> 00:24:59.539
English scientist Francis Crick is notable
not just for helping to discover the structure

00:24:59.539 --> 00:25:03.740
of DNA, but for his theory of ‘Directed
Panspermia.’

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:08.390
This hypothesis holds that life on earth might
have sprung from ‘seeds’ spread by an

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:10.630
ancient interplanetary civilization.

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:15.231
While there is little evidence such a thing
happened on Earth, within this universe, such

00:25:15.231 --> 00:25:20.940
an idea was eventually put into practice on
newly discovered, Earth-like worlds.

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:25.190
Numerous barren planets were intentionally
seeded with Earth species.

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:30.429
Isolated across lightyears of space, these
biospheres became home to spectacular forms

00:25:30.429 --> 00:25:32.570
of new life.

00:25:32.570 --> 00:25:37.320
This camo whale is a perfect example of how
strange life can get.

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:42.799
He hails our whale as he swims past, but the
youth only replies with fear.

00:25:42.799 --> 00:25:45.679
The youngster is reaching a point of crisis.

00:25:45.679 --> 00:25:50.390
His innate curiosity dulled, he no longer
finds joy in the unknown.

00:25:50.390 --> 00:25:55.440
Even the dazzling bioluminescence of Lamplighter
fish yields no reaction.

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:57.980
But perhaps not all is lost.

00:25:57.980 --> 00:26:01.420
Another youth has caught a Lamplighter, and
shows it off to our whale.

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:04.890
And for once, our whale doesn’t flee from
the encounter.

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:08.610
Instead, he seems excited to have made a new
acquaintance.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:13.300
His sudden sociability is unexpected, but
it might have to do with the fact that young

00:26:13.300 --> 00:26:16.510
whales can’t pass partner organisms to their
peers.

00:26:16.510 --> 00:26:20.090
Whereas an adult could give him a permanent
symbiosis.

00:26:20.090 --> 00:26:23.799
Perhaps all our whale really needed was a
friend he could be himself around.

00:26:23.799 --> 00:26:28.480
The two whales spend the rest of the day in
each other’s company.

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:29.919
Night comes to the reef.

00:26:29.919 --> 00:26:32.590
But our whale is up past his usual bedtime.

00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:36.120
He’s out with a pod of other adolescent
Brillos.

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:38.950
They’re here for a rare spectacle.

00:26:38.950 --> 00:26:43.040
Specks of light from Lamplighter fish illuminate
the nearby darkness.

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:47.410
This bold mating display makes them easy to
spot, but the Lamplighters don’t seem to

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:50.080
care about the young whales gobbling them
up.

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:55.130
It’s an example of a strategy known as ‘Predator
Satiation,’ where prey animals gather in

00:26:55.130 --> 00:27:00.200
such high numbers, they know there’s a low
chance of them being eaten, so they let predators

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:01.990
have their fill.

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:06.520
Completely full and surrounded by glowing
lights, our whale appears truly happy to be

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:09.770
spending a night with friends.

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:13.210
A new dawn, seemingly the same as any other.

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:16.010
But today, the tide is particularly low.

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:19.629
There are all sorts of interesting things
in the shallows.

00:27:19.629 --> 00:27:22.870
. . If one isn’t concerned about getting
stuck.

00:27:22.870 --> 00:27:26.320
There’s no more time for exploring.

00:27:26.320 --> 00:27:30.460
Trying to escape the dropping water level,
our whale becomes caught in the current of

00:27:30.460 --> 00:27:31.920
the trenches.

00:27:31.920 --> 00:27:37.399
One waterslide later, and he is unceremoniously
dumped into uncharted waters.

00:27:37.399 --> 00:27:43.050
He’s now out past the Great Reef — a dangerous
yet intriguing prospect.

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:44.220
And he’s not alone.

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:46.860
Something is watching from within a nearby
cave.

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:49.420
It’s a solitary Brillo whale.

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:53.570
Our whale swims closer, although something
seems off.

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:56.450
This recluse has been exiled from the herd.

00:27:56.450 --> 00:28:01.429
Banishment is rare in Brillo culture, and
was likely the result of a mating joust that

00:28:01.429 --> 00:28:02.480
turned deadly.

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:06.929
Years of painful isolation has made him truly
dangerous.

00:28:06.929 --> 00:28:09.559
Our Brillo says hello once more.

00:28:09.559 --> 00:28:10.559
Still no response.

00:28:10.559 --> 00:28:15.549
A helm made from a shark’s jaw indicates
the hermit’s fearsome might - but his secret

00:28:15.549 --> 00:28:18.630
weapon hides in a watchtower atop his armor.

00:28:18.630 --> 00:28:24.070
It’s a Vorpix Octopus, and it senses death
is on the menu.

00:28:24.070 --> 00:28:27.250
He acts as the exile whale’s hunting partner.

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:31.571
Normally he’d assist in ensnaring prey,
but when the target is as helpless as our

00:28:31.571 --> 00:28:34.110
whale, he prefers to watch.

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:35.740
Nothing like a front-row seat.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:38.640
A strong emotion overpowers our whale.

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:41.070
Is it fear at last?

00:28:41.070 --> 00:28:42.500
Evidently not.

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:48.150
Instead of fleeing, he’s playing and calling
to the exile as if he’s found a new friend.

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:52.530
But perhaps even more remarkably, the exile
isn’t attacking.

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:58.029
After decades of loneliness, this sudden compassion
seems to have profoundly moved him.

00:28:58.029 --> 00:29:02.190
He strains to remember a Brillo song that
isn’t a menacing warning.

00:29:02.190 --> 00:29:05.740
It’s not quite the show the octopus was
hoping to watch.

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:07.390
But he seems to have an idea.

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:13.240
At over four centuries old, this cephalopod
is highly intelligent, and knows a considerable

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:15.809
amount about Brillo biology.

00:29:15.809 --> 00:29:21.640
Swimming closer, he offers our Brillo a piece
of the exile whale’s symbiotic armor plating.

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:25.990
One swift motion — and our whale’s life
is changed forever.

00:29:25.990 --> 00:29:32.600
Now that he has bonded with a partner species,
over the coming years, our whale will change

00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:34.769
in remarkable ways.

00:29:34.769 --> 00:29:38.799
But for now, the first phase of the documentary
is complete.

00:29:38.799 --> 00:29:43.870
When we next check in with our whale, he might
have metamorphized into something unrecognizable

00:29:43.870 --> 00:29:47.840
— but that won’t be for quite some time.

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:51.850
That concludes all the chapters of “South
Scrimshaw” released so far.

00:29:51.850 --> 00:29:56.260
But creator Nathan O. Marsh is planning more
entries in the future.

00:29:56.260 --> 00:30:01.059
In the meantime, the full game is free to
play on Steam, which I’d highly recommend,

00:30:01.059 --> 00:30:05.930
as the full experience contains more worldbuilding
details than this video could cover.

00:30:05.930 --> 00:30:10.750
There are also links below to support the
creator directly and follow him on social

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:12.750
media.

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:15.029
As always, thanks for watching.

00:30:15.029 --> 00:30:19.790
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:30:19.790 --> 00:30:24.260
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:45.629
See you in the next video.

